  "response": "I’d request changes before approving the PR. `tomorrowMs` is incorrect if “tomorrow” means the same local wall-clock time on the next calendar day.\n\nThe finding’s evidence is:\n\n> “+ 86400000 (day) @L52”  \n> “lines: 52”  \n> “use a calendar-aware library (Luxon, Temporal, date-fns-tz) for day math”\n\nIt fails around the two annual DST transitions:\n\n- Spring-forward date: the local day is typically 23 hours, so adding 24 hours lands one hour late.\n- Fall-back date: the local day is typically 25 hours, so adding 24 hours lands one hour early.\n\nThe exact dates depend on the timezone and year—for example, in most US zones in 2026 they are March 8 and November 1. A calendar-aware “add one day” operation with an explicit timezone should replace the millisecond arithmetic. If the intended contract is instead “exactly 24 hours later,” the implementation is valid, but the helper should be renamed to express that.",
